        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "I can see all issues with similar requests are getting marked duplicate/not planned, but I think these suggestions are still important. The majority of the use case for mini.{ai,surround} for me and probably most programmers is simple brace/paren/bracket (especially for mini.surround). Vim already has a default behavior for `i)` `ib` `ab` etc text objects which works well. \n\nIf a user just wants mini.surround to parallel this muscle memory/methodology, they now have inconsistent behavior if they also enable mini.ai and mini.ai handles the bracket text objects and their aliases. Now the user is forced into either (1) disabling mini.ai for those text objects and having inconsistency between surround motions and a/i motions, or (2) keep mini.ai enabled and make both use a search_method which breaks the vim convention. This is a similar pain point to what is [shared by a user in the previous discussion](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/2346#issuecomment-4188715891). \n\nJust my 2cents regarding some of the [previous justification for existing behavior](https://github.com/nvim-mini/mini.nvim/issues/1501#issuecomment-2602209058)\n> * Although I do find sensible to skip preliminary \"next\" search in current line before whole neighborhood _in this particular example_, overall experience proved to be less positive (at least for me) in everyday usage. Mostly because covering region is not always so close an obvious and can even be out of screen. So common case of type `i)` and expect to select next visible pair of parenthesis can fail by very unexpectedly selecting much bigger region.\n\nFor this argument, would it not be equally fair to suggest using the `in`/`il`/`csn`/`csl` next/last binds when assuming/guessing next/last in a \"cover\"-ed scenario? And if we wanted to differentiate the two behaviors instead of changing the default, that's where a new option would be needed. ",
